In this lesson we'll look at quadrilaterals and other polygons, and how to sort them by name. A quadrilateral is just any closed shape with four straight sides, and the fun part is that some of these shapes share properties with others. A parallelogram has opposite sides parallel and equal, and its opposite angles match. A rectangle is a parallelogram that adds four right angles, while a rhombus keeps the parallelogram base but makes all four sides equal. A square is the special case where both happen together, all sides equal and all angles right. That's our inclusion chain, and it means every square is a rectangle, every rectangle is a parallelogram, but not the other way around. Then there's the trapezium, with exactly one pair of parallel sides, and the kite, with two pairs of adjacent equal sides. Beyond four sides, we name polygons by side count, like pentagon with five, hexagon with six, and octagon with eight, and the angles inside always add up to two hundred, three hundred and sixty, or more depending on the shape. Quick tip, don't trust a shape's looks alone, always check the side lengths and angles before naming it. Now let's see it in action.